title: Snowflake / UNC5537
page: breaches/snowflake-unc5537.html
as-of: 2026-11-23
promise: With credentials from infostealer logs, UNC5537 logged into victim Snowflake tenants, none of which required MFA, and took large datasets; about 165 organisations were notified as potentially exposed.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: ~165 | organisations notified as potentially exposed | by Mandiant and Snowflake
stat: 560M | Ticketmaster records, by the seller's claim | offered on BreachForums for $500,000
stat: AT&T | nearly all wireless customers' call and text records | Santander Bank was also a victim
Mandiant and Snowflake found no evidence Snowflake's platform was breached; the page says the attack was entirely predicated on absent MFA and reused credentials.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with, and with whose figure each one is.

Mandiant and Snowflake notified about 165 organisations as potentially exposed. That counts organisations notified, and the page does not say how many of them were compromised. Keep the words about and potentially exposed when you say it.

Victims included Ticketmaster, with 560M records by the seller's claim. That figure comes from the BreachForums listing, where the Ticketmaster database was offered for $500,000. Keep the seller's claim attached to it.

For AT&T, the data was the call and text records of nearly all its wireless customers. Santander Bank was also among the victims.

The page names more. Advance Auto Parts and Neiman Marcus also confirmed data stolen from their Snowflake environments. Pure Storage said a third party reached a single Snowflake analytics workspace. And the Los Angeles Unified School District said school data held by a vendor was caught up in the attack.

The threat actor is tracked as UNC5537, which the page calls financially motivated and extortion-focused.

MFA is multi-factor authentication. Mandiant and Snowflake found no evidence that Snowflake's own platform was breached; the page says the attack was entirely predicated on absent MFA and reused credentials.

== What happened
layout: text
UNC5537 spent months harvesting Snowflake credentials from infostealer logs. It then logged into victim tenants, none of which required MFA, and exfiltrated large datasets. The page dates the incident April to June 2024.
notes:
The page gives one date range for the incident, April to June 2024, and no date for any single step, so this deck has no timeline.

A financially motivated threat actor tracked as UNC5537 spent months harvesting Snowflake credentials from infostealer malware logs. Then it systematically logged into victim Snowflake tenants, none of which required MFA, and exfiltrated large datasets. A tenant here is one customer's own Snowflake account.

Mandiant and Snowflake notified about 165 potentially exposed organisations. Victims included Ticketmaster, AT&T and Santander Bank.

Mandiant and Snowflake found no evidence that Snowflake's own platform was breached. The page says the attack was entirely predicated on absent MFA and reused credentials.

The next slide sorts the page's figures and findings by whose they are.

== Who says what, per the page
layout: table
row: Claim | Whose it is
row: About 165 organisations potentially exposed | Mandiant and Snowflake, who notified them
row: 560M Ticketmaster records | The seller, in the BreachForums listing
row: Some credentials years old, still valid | Confirmed by Mandiant
row: Members in North America; an additional member in Turkey | Mandiant's assessment, with moderate confidence
row: No evidence Snowflake's own platform was breached | Mandiant and Snowflake's finding
The page does not say how many of the 165 were compromised.
notes:
The page is careful about whose figure or finding each one is. Keep its words when you retell these.

About 165 is the number of organisations Mandiant and Snowflake notified as potentially exposed. The page does not say how many of them were compromised.

The 560M Ticketmaster records is the seller's claim, from the BreachForums listing that offered the database for $500,000.

That some credentials were years old and still valid, because passwords had never been rotated, is something Mandiant confirmed.

That UNC5537 comprises members based in North America and collaborates with an additional member in Turkey is Mandiant's assessment, made with moderate confidence.

That there is no evidence Snowflake's own platform was breached is Mandiant and Snowflake's finding. That the attack was entirely predicated on absent MFA and reused credentials, the page states in its own summary.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Credential Acquisition | Infostealer logs | T1555.003
step: 02 | Initial Access | Valid credentials, no MFA | T1078.004
step: 03 | Discovery | Native SQL maps the data | T1619
step: 04 | Exfiltration | Temporary stage, then GET | T1530
step: 05 | Actions on Objective | Extortion | T1657
Arrows follow the page's stage order, for the campaign as a whole.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's five stages, each with its phase and its ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, Initial Credential Acquisition, is the harvesting of Snowflake credentials from infostealer logs. Stage 02, Initial Access, is the sign-in with those credentials, with no MFA challenge. Stage 03, Discovery, is mapping every database, schema and table the account could reach, with native SQL. Stage 04, Exfiltration, is copying the data into temporary stages with COPY INTO and downloading it with GET. Stage 05, under the page's Actions on Objective phase, is extortion: samples sent to victims, a demand for payment, and a listing on BreachForums when victims did not pay.

The page describes each stage for the campaign as a whole, with Ticketmaster as its example at stages 04 and 05. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Credentials came from infostealer logs, some of them years old
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Credential Acquisition
technique: T1555.003 | Credentials from Password Stores: Credentials from Web Browsers
fact: Malware | RedLine, Vidar and Lumma, on contractor and employee devices
fact: Taken | saved browser credentials, including Snowflake login URLs
fact: Logs from | underground markets, or prior campaigns
fact: Why it worked | no MFA requirement, no IP allowlist, no anomalous-login alerts
Mandiant confirmed some credentials were years old and still valid, never rotated.
notes:
Stage 01 is where the credentials came from. The page maps it to T1555.003, Credentials from Web Browsers.

UNC5537 sourced valid Snowflake credentials from infostealer malware logs. The RedLine, Vidar and Lumma stealer families had infected contractor and employee devices and exfiltrated saved browser credentials, including Snowflake login URLs. The logs were purchased from underground markets or obtained from prior campaigns.

Some logs dated from years before the campaign. Mandiant confirmed some credentials were years old and still valid, because passwords had never been rotated.

The page lists three reasons this worked: no Snowflake-side MFA requirement, no network policy allowlisting, and no alerting on anomalous logins. All three come back at stage 02, and the first two are defenses later in the deck.

The next slide explains infostealers in general terms.

== What an infostealer takes
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, Infostealer | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-infostealer
credit: Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1555.003 (attack.mitre.org)
An infostealer is commodity malware that scrapes browser-saved credentials, session cookies and tokens off an infected workstation; its logs are sold on criminal markets.

Browsers typically store saved credentials encrypted, ATT&CK notes, but methods exist to extract them.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary and MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

The glossary defines an infostealer as commodity malware that scrapes browser-saved credentials, session cookies, crypto wallets and authenticated SaaS tokens off an infected workstation. The logs are then sold on criminal markets. It adds that this is why an infected laptop is now a cloud problem.

ATT&CK's T1555.003, Credentials from Web Browsers, is a sub-technique of Credentials from Password Stores. Web browsers commonly save credentials such as website usernames and passwords so that they do not need to be entered manually. ATT&CK says browsers typically store them in an encrypted format within a credential store, but methods exist to extract plaintext credentials. After acquiring them, adversaries may try to recycle the credentials across different systems and accounts to expand access.

Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1555.003: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1555/003/

== The harvested credentials signed in directly; no compromised account had MFA
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: MFA | opt-in, and not enforced by default at the time
fact: Network policy | no IP allowlist on any of the affected tenants
fact: Tools | SnowSQL CLI, Snowflake JDBC driver, custom automation scripts
fact: Detection gap | logins from new IPs or countries raised no owner alert
On every victim account, valid credentials plus no MFA was enough to sign in.
notes:
Stage 02 is the sign-in. The page maps it to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts.

Using the harvested credentials, UNC5537 authenticated to each victim's Snowflake instance. Snowflake did not enforce MFA by default at the time; it was available but opt-in at account level. None of the compromised accounts had MFA enabled, and no network policy restricted which IPs could connect to the tenants.

The attacker connected with the SnowSQL CLI, Snowflake's command-line client, and the Snowflake JDBC driver, to automate credential testing at scale. JDBC, Java Database Connectivity, is the standard way Java programs connect to a database.

Logins from new IPs or countries generated no alert to account owners.

The page's verdict comes in its first defense: every victim account was compromised solely because valid credentials plus no MFA was sufficient to authenticate.

The next slide explains valid cloud accounts and MFA in general terms.

== Valid cloud accounts, and what MFA means
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1078.004 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078/004/
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entry MFA
ATT&CK's T1078.004 covers adversaries using valid cloud accounts: accounts an organization creates for users, services or administration in a cloud service or SaaS application.

The csoh.org glossary defines MFA as something you know plus something you have, or are.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK and the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes cloud accounts as those created and configured by an organization for use by users, remote support, services, or for administration of resources within a cloud service provider or SaaS application. They can exist solely in the cloud, or be synced or federated with other identity sources.

The glossary defines MFA, multi-factor authentication, as something you know plus something you have, or something you are.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-mfa

== Native SQL commands mapped every table the compromised user could reach
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Discovery
technique: T1619 | Cloud Storage Object Discovery
fact: Commands | SHOW DATABASES, SHOW SCHEMAS, SHOW TABLES
fact: Also used | SELECT * FROM INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES
fact: Account types | often service or analyst, with broad SELECT permissions
fact: Typical finding | PII: names, emails, phone numbers, payment card data, SSNs
No exploitation needed: the tenant's full data landscape was visible in seconds.
notes:
Stage 03 is mapping the data. The page maps it to T1619, Cloud Storage Object Discovery.

Once authenticated, UNC5537 ran Snowflake's native enumeration commands, SHOW DATABASES, SHOW SCHEMAS and SHOW TABLES, to identify all databases, schemas and tables accessible to the compromised user. It also used SELECT star from INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES to enumerate accessible objects. INFORMATION_SCHEMA is the set of views a database provides to describe its own objects.

The commands are native SQL and require no exploitation. Because the accounts were often service accounts or analyst accounts with broad SELECT permissions, the full data landscape of the tenant was visible in seconds.

The typical finding, the page says, was PII tables: customer names, emails, phone numbers, payment card data and Social Security numbers. PII is personally identifiable information.

== Data was copied into temporary stages, then downloaded with GET
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1530 | Data from Cloud Storage
fact: Staging | CREATE TEMPORARY STAGE, then COPY INTO it as GZIP files
fact: Exit | GET to the attacker's machine; GET reads internal stages only
fact: Mandiant saw | ALEXHOST VPS exfiltration; data stored on VPS providers, MEGA
fact: Ticketmaster | 560M records and 1.3TB by the seller's claim; multiple sessions
Query history kept the commands for later forensics; no real-time alert fired.
notes:
Stage 04 is getting the data out. The page maps it to T1530, Data from Cloud Storage.

UNC5537 used standard Snowflake SQL. Mandiant lists the commands: SELECT star to download individual tables, LIST to enumerate other stages, CREATE TEMPORARY STAGE, COPY INTO to copy table data into those temporary stages as GZIP-compressed files, and finally GET to download the files to directories on the attacker's machine. A stage, in Snowflake, is a named location for data files. A temporary stage is deleted when the session that created it ends.

Snowflake's GET command downloads only from internal stages, so these temporary stages were internal ones, and GET was the step that took the data out. Mandiant saw exfiltration through VPS systems, virtual private servers, from ALEXHOST, a Moldovan provider, and stolen data stored on several VPS providers and on MEGA, a cloud storage service.

Large datasets, such as the Ticketmaster data the seller claimed held 560M records, were extracted over multiple sessions. The 1.3TB is the seller's claim too.

Query history retained these commands, providing forensic visibility after the fact, but no real-time alerting fired during exfiltration. The page lists two detection gaps: no DLP, data loss prevention, on COPY INTO, and no alert on temporary stage creation or GET downloads. Query history comes back in the third defense.

== UNC5537 demanded payment for deletion; unpaid victims' data was listed for sale
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Actions on Objective
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Contact | victims directly, with samples of stolen data as proof
fact: Demand | payment for deletion
fact: If unpaid | datasets listed for sale on BreachForums
fact: Ticketmaster | offered for $500,000; 560M records by the seller's claim
The page calls UNC5537 financially motivated and extortion-focused.
notes:
Stage 05 is the extortion. The page maps it to T1657, Financial Theft.

After exfiltration, UNC5537 contacted victims directly with samples of stolen data as proof, demanding payment for deletion. When victims did not pay, datasets were listed for sale on BreachForums, a cybercrime forum. The Ticketmaster database was offered for $500,000, with 560M records by the seller's claim.

Mandiant assesses with moderate confidence that UNC5537 comprises members based in North America and collaborates with an additional member in Turkey. Keep the attribution and the moderate confidence when you say it.

The page does not say how many victims paid, or how many datasets were listed.

No defense on the page is tied to this stage. That gap is one of the discussion questions at the end.

The next slide explains what ATT&CK means by financial theft.

== What ATT&CK calls financial theft
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1657 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1657/
ATT&CK's T1657 covers adversaries stealing monetary resources through extortion, social engineering, technical theft or other methods, for their own financial gain at the expense of victims.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes Financial Theft as adversaries stealing monetary resources from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft or other methods aimed at their own financial gain, at the expense of the availability of these resources for victims.

It calls financial theft the ultimate objective of several popular campaign types, including business email compromise and fraud, bank hacking and exploiting cryptocurrency networks.

ATT&CK's description gives ransomware as its example of extortion, where payment is demanded after data is encrypted and exfiltrated. The page describes no encryption in this campaign, so do not read that example into it.

== Mandiant and Snowflake found no evidence of a Snowflake platform breach
layout: text
The page says the attack was entirely predicated on absent MFA and reused credentials.
- Sign-in: valid credentials; no compromised account had MFA.
- Discovery: native SQL commands that require no exploitation.
- Exfiltration: standard Snowflake SQL, from temporary stage to GET.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws in its summary. Mandiant and Snowflake found no evidence that Snowflake's own platform was breached, and the page says the attack was entirely predicated on absent MFA and reused credentials.

The stages inside Snowflake bear that out. Stage 02 used valid credentials, and none of the compromised accounts had MFA enabled. Stage 03 used Snowflake's native enumeration commands, which the page says are native SQL and require no exploitation. At stage 04, UNC5537 used standard Snowflake SQL: a temporary stage, COPY INTO and GET.

The defenses come next, starting with how each one lines up against the stages.

== Five defenses against five stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05
row: Strong authentication for every user | 02
row: Network policies allowlisting source IPs | 02
row: Watch query history for staging and download | 04
row: Least-privilege Snowflake roles | 03
row: Credential-exposure monitoring | 01
No defense on the page is tied to stage 05, the extortion.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's five defenses against the five stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something only that stage has.

Strong authentication answers stage 02: the page says every victim account was compromised solely because valid credentials plus no MFA was sufficient to authenticate. Network policies answer stage 02 as well, where no IP allowlist was configured on any affected tenant; the defense says a login from a residential or VPS IP should be impossible. Watching query history for the staging and download sequence answers stage 04: the temporary stage, COPY INTO, GET and the SELECT star table downloads are stage 04's (stage 03's enumeration also uses SELECT star, on INFORMATION_SCHEMA). Least-privilege roles answer stage 03, where the accounts were often service or analyst accounts with broad SELECT permissions. Credential-exposure monitoring answers stage 01: infostealer logs on underground markets.

Stage 01's own step lists no MFA requirement and no network policy allowlisting among the reasons it worked. Both of those defenses name the sign-in, so the matrix marks them at stage 02.

No defense on the page is tied to stage 05, the extortion. That gap is the second discussion question.

== Require strong authentication for every user, human and service
layout: compare
col: Human users | MFA, for those who sign in with a password
col: Service users | No password sign-in | Move to a method such as workload identity federation or key-pair authentication
col: Reader and trial accounts | Outside the rollout | Check these yourself
Snowflake's phased rollout is scheduled to finish in late 2026. The page says this single control would have stopped the entire campaign.
notes:
The first defense answers stage 02. The page says this single control would have stopped the entire campaign, because every victim account was compromised solely because valid credentials plus no MFA was sufficient to authenticate.

Snowflake's phased rollout, scheduled to finish in late 2026, requires MFA for human users who sign in with a password. It blocks password sign-in for service users, which must move to a method such as workload identity federation or key-pair authentication. Workload identity federation lets a workload authenticate with a short-lived token from its own identity provider instead of a stored secret. Key-pair authentication signs in with a private key instead of a password.

Reader and trial accounts are outside that rollout, so check those yourself. Bring every other user into line before your account's enforcement date rather than after it.

== Allowlist source IPs, and grant roles only the schemas they need
layout: compare
col: Network policies (stage 02) | NETWORK POLICY objects restrict which IP ranges can connect | Allow corporate egress IPs, VPN ranges, known analytics tool IPs | A login from a residential or VPS IP should be impossible
col: Least-privilege roles (stage 03) | Analyst and service accounts: READ access only to the schemas they need | Not SELECT * on the entire data warehouse | No ACCOUNTADMIN for applications or individual analysts
No affected tenant had an IP allowlist, and the accounts often had broad SELECT permissions.
notes:
Two defenses work on who can connect, and what they can see once connected.

For stage 02, configure Snowflake network policies to allowlist known source IPs. Snowflake NETWORK POLICY objects restrict which IP ranges can connect to a tenant. Lock production Snowflake access to your corporate egress IPs, VPN ranges and known analytics tool IPs. The page says a login from a residential or VPS IP, a rented virtual private server, should be impossible. At stage 02, no IP allowlist was configured on any of the affected tenants.

For stage 03, apply least-privilege Snowflake roles. Analyst accounts and service accounts should have READ access only to the specific schemas they need, not SELECT star on the entire data warehouse. Use Snowflake's role hierarchy, and avoid granting ACCOUNTADMIN, Snowflake's top administrative role, to any account used by applications or individual analysts. At stage 03, those accounts often had broad SELECT permissions, and the tenant's full data landscape was visible in seconds.

== Watch the staging and download sequence, and infostealer logs
layout: compare
col: Query history (stage 04) | Alert when CREATE TEMPORARY STAGE, COPY INTO and GET appear together for unexpected users | Treat large SELECT * downloads from an unfamiliar IP or application the same way
col: Credential exposure (stage 01) | Infostealer logs with your credentials appear on underground markets before attacks | Services like SpyCloud, Hudson Rock or Flare detect them | Alerts trigger forced password resets and MFA re-enrolment
Query history is the page's most reliable detection signal for this attack pattern.
notes:
Two defenses are about seeing the attack coming, or seeing it happen.

For stage 04, monitor Snowflake QUERY_HISTORY for the staging and download sequence. As Mandiant describes it, the attacker created a temporary stage, used COPY INTO to fill it with compressed table data, then ran GET to download the files. A temporary stage is deleted when its session ends, so all three commands share one session. Alert when they appear together for a user or client that does not normally run them, and treat large SELECT star downloads from an unfamiliar IP or application the same way. Mandiant's report links a Snowflake threat hunting guide with queries for these steps. A COPY INTO to an external stage that is not a known data pipeline target should also trigger an immediate alert. Enable Access History, and query QUERY_HISTORY in your SIEM, your security information and event management system. The page calls this the most reliable detection signal for this attack pattern.

For stage 01, subscribe to credential-exposure monitoring services. Infostealer logs containing your organisation's credentials appear on underground markets before attacks. The page names SpyCloud, Hudson Rock and Flare as examples of services that detect them. Integrate their alerts to trigger forced password resets and MFA re-enrolment.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Does every Snowflake user, including reader and trial accounts, use strong authentication?
check: Do network policies limit access to egress IPs, VPN ranges and analytics tool IPs?
check: Would an unexpected user's CREATE TEMPORARY STAGE, COPY INTO and GET raise an alert?
check: Would large SELECT * downloads from an unfamiliar IP or application stand out?
check: Is Access History enabled, with QUERY_HISTORY queried in your SIEM?
check: Do analyst and service accounts read only the schemas they need, without ACCOUNTADMIN?
check: Would you know if your credentials showed up in infostealer logs?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether every Snowflake user, human and service, signs in with strong authentication today, including reader and trial accounts, which are outside Snowflake's rollout.

Ask whether network policies limit access to your corporate egress IPs, VPN ranges and known analytics tool IPs, so that a login from a residential or VPS IP is impossible.

Ask whether CREATE TEMPORARY STAGE, COPY INTO and GET together, from a user or client that does not normally run them, would raise an alert, and whether large SELECT star downloads from an unfamiliar IP or application would. Ask whether Access History is enabled with QUERY_HISTORY queried in your SIEM.

Ask whether analyst and service accounts have READ access only to the schemas they need, and whether any application or individual analyst holds ACCOUNTADMIN.

Finally, ask whether you would know if your organisation's credentials showed up in infostealer logs, and whether that would trigger a forced password reset and MFA re-enrolment.

== Would anything have alerted while the data left?
layout: question
At stage 02, logins from new IPs or countries raised no alert. At stage 04, query history retained the commands, but no real-time alert fired.

Name the first alert in your environment that would have fired, and its stage.
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page names a detection gap at each of these stages. At stage 02, logins from new IPs or countries generated no alert to account owners. At stage 04, Snowflake's query history log retained the commands, providing forensic visibility after the fact, but no real-time alerting fired during exfiltration. The page also lists no DLP on COPY INTO and no alert on temporary stage creation or GET downloads.

Ask each person to name one specific alert from their own environment, and the stage it would catch, before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says about detection.

== What the page says about detection
layout: answer
Watch QUERY_HISTORY, the page's most reliable detection signal for this attack pattern.
- Alert on CREATE TEMPORARY STAGE, COPY INTO and GET together.
- Treat large SELECT * downloads from unfamiliar IPs or applications the same way.
notes:
The page's answer is to monitor Snowflake QUERY_HISTORY for the staging and download sequence, and it calls this the most reliable detection signal for this attack pattern. As Mandiant describes it, the attacker created a temporary stage, filled it with COPY INTO and ran GET to download the files, all in one session. Alert when those commands appear together for a user or client that does not normally run them, and treat large SELECT star downloads from an unfamiliar IP or application the same way. Enable Access History, and query the QUERY_HISTORY view in your SIEM.

Credential-exposure monitoring looks earlier. Infostealer logs containing your organisation's credentials appear on underground markets before attacks, and the page says to integrate those alerts to trigger forced password resets and MFA re-enrolment.

For the logins themselves, the page's defense is prevention rather than an alert: a network policy under which a login from a residential or VPS IP should be impossible.

Bring the group's examples back to this. If the first alert they named would have come only after the data left, the page points at the commands that took it out: the staging and download sequence.

== What would you add for stage 05?
layout: question
The page ties defenses to stages 01 to 04, and none to stage 05: samples sent to victims, a payment demand, a BreachForums listing when unpaid.

What would you add, and which detail would it change?
notes:
This question comes from the gap in the defense matrix.

The page offers defenses tied to stages 01, 02, 03 and 04. It ties nothing to stage 05, the extortion: UNC5537 contacted victims directly with samples of stolen data as proof, demanding payment for deletion, and when victims did not pay, datasets were listed for sale on BreachForums.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have changed.

== The page ties no defense to stage 05
layout: answer
The page ties no defense to the extortion, and this deck adds none. Test any idea against stage 05's facts: samples as proof, payment demanded for deletion, and a BreachForums listing when victims did not pay.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 05, and this deck adds none of its own.

Test each idea the group proposes against the facts the page reports for stage 05. UNC5537 contacted victims directly with samples of stolen data as proof, demanding payment for deletion. When victims did not pay, datasets were listed for sale on BreachForums, and the Ticketmaster database was offered for $500,000. Mandiant assesses with moderate confidence that UNC5537 comprises members based in North America and collaborates with an additional member in Turkey.

Some proposals will reach back to earlier stages. The page says strong authentication would have stopped the entire campaign; the defense's own words name the sign-in, which is why the matrix marks it at stage 02. Treat any control the group proposes for the extortion itself as the group's own suggestion.

